<!--
    GH-9184: "FMA YMM definitions use 128-bit temp instead of 256-bit"
    https://github.com/NationalSecurityAgency/ghidra/issues/9184

    In x86 fma.sinc every 256-bit YMM/$(VEX_L256) FMA constructor declared its
    pcodeop result in a 128-bit temp (`local tmp:16`) and then `ZmmReg1 =
    zext(tmp)`.  Because the YMM operation is 256 bits wide, writing into a
    16-byte temp truncated lanes 4-7 (ps) / 2-3 (pd) of every result.  The
    decompiler renders the accumulator as `undefined1 [16]` and inserts a
    per-iteration `ZEXT1632` truncation round-trip feeding the next FMA.

    Fix (kuna GH-9184): widen the YMM temps to `local tmp:32`; the
    XMM/$(VEX_L128) forms correctly keep `tmp:16`.

    Bytes below are a `dot8_fma`-style 256-bit float accumulation loop:
      vxorps  ymm0,ymm0,ymm0
      xor     eax,eax
    .L:
      vmovups ymm1,[rdi+rax*4]
      vmovups ymm2,[rsi+rax*4]
      vfmadd231ps ymm0,ymm1,ymm2     ; c4 e2 75 b8 c2  (VEX.256.66.0F38.W0 B8)
      add     eax,8
      cmp     eax,edx
      jl      .L
      vmovups [rcx],ymm0
      vzeroupper
      ret

    Stage: S1/S2 decode + pcode-semantics (SLEIGH spec-fix, no option).  This
    testcase asserts the FIXED width: the FMA accumulator is a 256-bit value
    (undefined1 [32]) with NO per-iteration ZEXT1632 truncation, and forbids
    the buggy `undefined1 [16]` accumulator.
-->
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x100000" readonly="true">
c5fc57c031c0c5fc100c87c5fc101486c4e275b8c283c00839d07ceac5fc1101c5f877c3
</bytechunk>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x100000" name="dot8_fma"/>
</binaryimage>
<script>
  <com>lo fu dot8_fma</com>
  <com>dec</com>
  <com>print C</com>
  <com>quit</com>
</script>
<stringmatch name="GH9184 #1: FMA accumulator is 256-bit (undefined1 [32]), not truncated to [16]" min="1" max="1">xunknown1 \w+ \[32\];</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="GH9184 #2: no 128-bit accumulator temp (undefined1 [16])" min="0" max="0">xunknown1 \w+ \[16\];</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="GH9184 #3: no per-iteration ZEXT1632 truncation round-trip on the FMA result" min="0" max="0">ZEXT1632</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="GH9184 #4: the vfmadd231ps_fma pcodeop is still emitted (sanity)" min="1" max="1">vfmadd231ps_fma\(</stringmatch>
</decompilertest>
